WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.190
Hey, Thoughty 2 here.

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:07.570
Through science, our civilisation has developed
faster than anyone could have imagined and

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:14.150
we’ve made great leaps forward in our understanding
of the universe, our minds, nature and everything

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:15.150
else.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:19.689
But that journey has sometimes taken us in
a pretty weird direction, so today we look

00:00:19.689 --> 00:00:24.830
at the five most bizarre scientific experiments
ever conducted.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:34.180
The USA has the largest prison population
in the world, with over 2 million people behind

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:35.180
bars.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:39.290
They prop up a lot of American industries,
with prisoners making everything from books

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:45.610
of braille for the blind to lingerie to canoes;
all of which sounds like a rejected story

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:49.850
line from prison break; who needs a map of
the prison tattooed on your body when you’ve

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.630
got braille, a sexy bra and a paddle?

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:58.660
So with all these people locked up, we should
really know more about how incarceration affects

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:04.809
us and how it affects the guards because,
as Alan Moore asked in his award winning comic;

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:06.969
“Who watches the watchmen?”

00:01:06.969 --> 00:01:10.770
This was the purpose of the Stanford Prison
Experiment.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:19.659
For 6 days in 1971, psychology professor Philip
Zimbardo paid 24 volunteers $15 a day to play

00:01:19.659 --> 00:01:22.360
the roles of guards and prisoners.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:26.840
He was aiming to look at the causes of abusive
behaviour; was it down to the personality

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:31.010
of the people involved or was it down to the
system itself?

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:35.399
The prisoners and guards were given appropriate
uniforms and prisoners were arrested from

00:01:35.399 --> 00:01:38.759
their homes and then put three to a cell.

00:01:38.759 --> 00:01:43.090
They were given a number and only referred
to by that number from then on.

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:47.819
Prisoners also had a chain around one ankle,
to remind them of their lack of freedom.

00:01:47.819 --> 00:01:50.549
The results were pretty shocking.

00:01:50.549 --> 00:01:59.469
Within just 36 hours, prisoner #8612 had to
be removed due to going into a wild rage.

00:01:59.469 --> 00:02:04.700
All in all, 5 prisoners had to be removed
early from the experiment and the whole program

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:10.430
was stopped after just six days; the plan
had been to go on for two weeks.

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:15.500
Some of the guards were actually disappointed
it got stopped early; they were beginning

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.140
to enjoy their roles.

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:23.590
In fact, one third displayed genuine sadistic
tendencies.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:29.060
Punishments included removing mattresses,
making prisoners be naked, not allowing them

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:33.670
to empty their toilet bucket, locking them
in cupboards and many more.

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:36.420
Power is a dangerous thing.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:41.440
Even Zimbardo was forced to admit that he
had lost perspective as the experimenter,

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:45.120
and was surprised by what he allowed to continue.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.150
Post-experiment, there were serious concerns
about the human rights violations carried

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:53.780
out during the experiment and Zimbardo even
stopped some of the prisoners from leaving

00:02:53.780 --> 00:03:00.080
the experiment early, after they had had enough,
even though he told them before it started,

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.930
that they could leave at any point.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:08.960
It was only the objections of his girlfriend
Christina Maslach that made him finish the

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:10.680
experiment early.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:13.620
She was mainly worried about the mental health
of the prisoners.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.850
Or maybe, she was just sick of him torturing
prisoners all day, instead of helping her

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:18.850
with the housework.

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:24.700
Our next experiment came about as a result
of a huge amount of physical labour; a gold

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:28.430
mine dug almost 2.5 kilometres into the earth.

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:35.140
The Homestake mine in Lead, South Dakota,
was started in 1876 and operated for over

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:39.180
100 years, finally stopping production in
2001.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:45.440
During this time, it produced over 1.25 million
kilograms of gold, along with some silver

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:46.440
as well.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:51.220
There’s still more gold down there but now
the mine is so deep that it was costing more

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:56.640
money to extract it than its sale price; kind
of the whole point of mining.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.760
As you know, scientists are basically like
moles and love doing stuff underground.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:07.210
So in 2007, they moved in and started looking
for something much more rarely seen than gold,

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:11.750
and far more valuable when it comes to the
mysteries of the universe.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:18.470
The Large Underground Xenon experiment is
set about 1.5 km underground and was designed

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:21.650
to find evidence of dark matter.

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:30.940
Dark Matter and Dark Energy make up around
95% of our universe so only 5% are the more

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:35.930
tangible atoms we deal with on a daily basis;
the air we breathe, the ground beneath your

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:40.720
feet, left over take away food, hats, you
know; stuff.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:47.160
In 1932, a physicist called Jan Oort realised
that the stars and planets in the milky way

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:53.520
were orbiting at the wrong speed, if they
are the only mass in the galaxy.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:58.300
He put forward the theory that there must
be something else that we can’t see that

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:01.420
is adding a huge amount of mass to the universe.

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:04.990
And so the idea of dark matter was born.

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:09.940
The problem with Dark Matter is, that, although
it’s everywhere, since it’s far more common

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:15.750
than anything else in the universe, even Starbucks,
it’s really really weak.

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:20.530
That means we never see it interact with any
normal matter.

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:25.160
The world we see around us is extremely noisy;
there are particles bouncing around all over

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:26.160
the place.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.650
Imagine you are holding a kid’s party and
you have 30 little kids, all full of cake,

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:32.020
sweets and destructive tendencies.

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:38.610
And now imagine, amongst all this chaos, you’re
trying to listen out for a tiny hamster, scratching

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:39.610
its nose.

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:41.380
You’ll just never hear it.

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:45.419
Plus, the hamster will probably get crushed,
or covered in paint or something.

00:05:45.419 --> 00:05:51.120
When you look up into space, it looks empty
but actually you are being bombarded with

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:56.260
cosmic rays, constantly; around 3 or 4 a second.

00:05:56.260 --> 00:06:00.620
So when you’re looking for something as
shy as dark matter, this constant attack will

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.790
make it impossible to find.

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:07.180
You need to block out the rays and what better
way to do that, than by building your lab

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:09.710
under a mile of thick rock.

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:15.720
Here the cosmic rays drop from 3 or 4 a second
to just a couple a month.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:22.420
The scientists took a 370 kg box of xenon,
an element that is extremely unreactive, and

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:25.169
put it into a big tank of water.

00:06:25.169 --> 00:06:29.110
Scientists call it “the quietest place in
the universe.”

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:33.310
So what have they found in 2 years of watching
this very quiet box?

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:34.310
Um… nothing.

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:37.290
But are they going to give up?

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:42.220
Of course not, this is science; they’re
going to build a bigger box.

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:47.910
Now if you think 2 years is a long time to
sit about while nothing happens then you are

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:52.680
in for a surprise; a very very slow and boring
surprise.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:58.510
The Pitch Drop Experiment is designed to show
that “pitch” is indeed a liquid.

00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:04.040
Pitch is a form of petrol, also known as asphalt
or bitumen, and although it looks like a solid

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:09.320
and you can smash it with a hammer, it’s
actually just an extremely viscous liquid,

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:12.070
like the thickest blackest honey you could
imagine.

00:07:12.070 --> 00:07:17.860
In 1927, at the University of Queensland,
Australia, Professor Thomas Parnell heated

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:20.949
some pitch and put it into a sealed funnel.

00:07:20.949 --> 00:07:26.389
He allowed it to settle for 3 years and then
cut off the tip of the funnel so the pitch

00:07:26.389 --> 00:07:27.740
could flow out.

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:32.760
And it did, in 1938, taking just 8 years.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:36.570
It’s a good thing it fell when it did or
the whole world might have been too distracted

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:40.620
watching the world’s most boring experiment,
to get on with the war.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:44.820
The professor didn’t get to see it drop
himself, as 8 years is quite a long time to

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:49.530
concentrate on something, so he left the experiment
alone to do its thing.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:55.401
The second drop took just one month more than
the last one but he missed it again, and so

00:07:55.401 --> 00:07:58.420
on it went, and it’s still going.

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:03.979
It now holds the record as the longest continuous
running experiment of all time.

00:08:03.979 --> 00:08:07.590
So what happened when he finally saw it drop?

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:10.300
Nothing, because he never did.

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:17.960
He sadly died in 2013 having missed every
one of the 8 drops the experiment had made.

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:24.520
In 1988, he saw that it was super close, went
to make a cup of tea and missed it.

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:30.250
In 2000, he was away but the wonders of modern
technology allowed him to set up a camera.

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:31.340
But guess what?

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:32.700
The camera malfunctioned.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:35.740
And we’re still waiting to see it.

00:08:35.740 --> 00:08:41.669
The 9th drop, in 2014, had stretched all the
way down to the base and it was broken when

00:08:41.669 --> 00:08:46.020
they tried to empty out the catchment dish,
so it was broken artificially and doesn’t

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:47.510
count as a drop.

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:52.290
The drops have taken longer since they installed
air conditioning, you know, to really ramp

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:53.690
up the tension.

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:58.070
What this really proves is that people will
keep doing something, no matter how boring

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:01.790
or irritating, just because they can.

00:09:01.790 --> 00:09:04.460
Just like level 350 of Candy Crush.

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:09.360
But if you want an experiment that really
shows what people are prepared to do, then

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:13.170
The Milgram Experiment is certainly one to
read up on.

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:18.060
In 1961, Adolf Eichmann, one of the Nazis
who orchestrated the Holocaust, was put on

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:19.540
trial for war crimes.

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:24.850
It was a hot topic at the time and one of
the big questions going around was “what

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:28.600
if he was just following orders, the same
as any other soldier?”

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:34.540
Well, Jewish psychologist Stanley Milgram,
wanted to put this to the test and he designed

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:39.020
an experiment that has given us great insight
into the relationship that people have with

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:40.690
authority.

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:45.200
Why is it, for example, that if a doctor told
you to take off your pants, you’d think

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:50.480
“okay”, but if the bus driver told you,
you’d probably be calling a hotline?

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:55.230
The Milgram experiment was conducted at Yale
university in July 1961.

00:09:55.230 --> 00:10:00.740
There were three roles, the experimenter (who
was the person of authority), the teacher

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:06.240
(who was the volunteer), and the learner (who
the teacher believed was another volunteer

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:08.320
but was actually an actor).

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:13.350
The teacher sat in one room with a microphone
and the learner sat in another room.

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:15.360
There was no window between the rooms.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:19.930
The teacher told the learner a word and then
gave a choice of four other words and the

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:21.870
learner had to choose the correct pair.

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:26.880
If the match was incorrect, the teacher pressed
a button and gave the learner a small electric

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:27.880
shock.

00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:32.110
At the beginning of the experiment, the teacher
was given a small test shock, to demonstrate

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:34.690
what the learner would be experiencing.

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:40.230
But every time the learner made a mistake,
the teacher had to increase the voltage.

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:44.270
The learner was an actor, of course, so there
were no real shocks.

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:50.029
But as the voltage increased, they would make
louder and louder noises and screams and complain

00:10:50.029 --> 00:10:53.880
about their heart condition, which had been
mentioned earlier on.

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:56.280
They would bang on the walls and beg to stop.

00:10:56.280 --> 00:11:02.290
If the voltage went over a lethal dose, around
300 volts, they were told to make no noise,

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:04.399
as if they had died.

00:11:04.399 --> 00:11:07.270
So, how high do you think the teachers went?

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:11.339
How many of the volunteers would refuse to
continue in their role as the teacher when

00:11:11.339 --> 00:11:14.200
they heard the pain of the learner?

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:23.649
Shockingly, 65% of teachers ended up giving
the maximum 450 volt shock; probably frying

00:11:23.649 --> 00:11:28.530
the corpse of their poor dead learner, if
the shocks were real.

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:29.660
How is this possible?

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:31.950
Do we live in a world full of psychopaths?

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:37.370
No, it’s all about the relationship of the
volunteer and the experimenter.

00:11:37.370 --> 00:11:42.790
Because the experimenter is there and telling
them to continue, most people feel like what

00:11:42.790 --> 00:11:45.250
they’re doing must be okay.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:48.380
We are taught to trust people in authority.

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:53.450
In variations of the test, they realised a
lot of things make a difference to this trust,

00:11:53.450 --> 00:11:59.500
such as being in the same room as the experimenter,
the type of clothing they wear and so on.

00:11:59.500 --> 00:12:03.220
Imagine you’re outside an electronics store
and there’s a TV in a box.

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:07.790
A guy walks up, points at the flat screen
TV and tells you “that’s yours, it’s

00:12:07.790 --> 00:12:11.640
free - go on, take it”, you’d probably
think he was a criminal.

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:18.180
But if he was in a police uniform, boom; you’d
be enjoying Netflix in 70 inches, quicker

00:12:18.180 --> 00:12:21.330
than you could say “The Milgram Experiment”.

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:24.990
Our final experiment takes us into the animal
kingdom.

00:12:24.990 --> 00:12:30.060
In 1962, as the swinging sixties were just
getting going, a group of scientists wondered

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:33.550
“what happens if you give an elephant acid?”

00:12:33.550 --> 00:12:37.339
I wonder if this question actually answers
another question; “what happens if you give

00:12:37.339 --> 00:12:41.800
a group of scientists acid?” because this
is probably how all of this madness came about

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:42.980
in the first place.

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:50.800
Warren Thomas, who was the director of Oklahoma
zoo, took 297 milligrams of LSD and shot it

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:53.269
into Tusko the Elephant.

00:12:53.269 --> 00:12:55.630
Did Tusko begin to taste colour?

00:12:55.630 --> 00:12:59.880
Did he begin to …like… understand the
universe, dude?

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:03.830
No, he went on the greatest trip of them all;
he died.

00:13:03.830 --> 00:13:09.170
He ran around for a couple of minutes, then
keeled over and was dead in an hour.

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:13.040
An important discovery was made; don’t give
acid to elephants.

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:17.170
The world is a more enlightened place, thanks
to poor Tusko.

00:13:17.170 --> 00:13:21.880
Actually, this isn’t the only case of animals
being given LSD around this time.

00:13:21.880 --> 00:13:26.890
A NASA funded project, led by John Lilly,
was set up to see if we could communicate

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:28.450
with dolphins.

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:33.770
Due to its reputation as a “mind expander”
they tried using acid but it didn’t seem

00:13:33.770 --> 00:13:35.770
to have any real effect.

00:13:35.770 --> 00:13:41.420
Interestingly, they also built a human/dolphin
cohabitation space so that a woman called

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:46.510
Margaret Lovatt could live with Peter the
dolphin and try to teach him English.

00:13:46.510 --> 00:13:48.860
The sixties were pretty weird, weren’t they?

00:13:48.860 --> 00:13:55.440
That’s the thing about science though; it
can seem pretty crazy, until you learn something

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:58.820
important, and then it’s not so crazy after
all.

00:13:58.820 --> 00:14:04.170
There is an idea that every great discovery
is an accident, since if you knew exactly

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:07.589
what you were looking for, then you wouldn’t
need to discover it.

00:14:07.589 --> 00:14:10.480
So I guess we’ll just have to let the madness
continue.

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:28.590
Thanks for the view, subscribe for more Thoughty2.

